                <h2>Case Background: 8% CTR, Zero Conversions After Three Weeks</h2>
<p>A utility app team running Meta ads in Southeast Asia had Creative A running for two weeks with a steady 7.8% CTR and a mere $1.2 CPM. The account model looked exceptionally healthy. The marketing staff was optimistic, believing they'd found a breakout creative.</p>
<p>The third-week review told a different story: over 120,000 clicks, zero conversions. The team began questioning Meta's algorithm, account权重, and even resorting to superstition.</p>
<p>It's not superstition. This creative was a textbook case of 'runnable but not sellable.'</p>
<h2>What 'Runnable' and 'Sellable' Actually Mean</h2>
<p>Before diving into the criteria, let's clarify these two concepts.</p>
<p><strong>Runnable</strong>: A creative that generates traffic, measured by CTR, CPM, and CPC. Runnable creatives mean the system is willing to push them and users are willing to click.</p>
<p><strong>Sellable</strong>: A creative that drives conversions, measured by CVR, CPA, and ROI. Sellable creatives mean users who enter are genuinely willing to complete the target action.</p>
<p>The gap between these two lies in what happens after the click—landing page experience, product match, and purchase decision-making.</p>
<h2>Three Typical Problem Scenarios and Diagnosis Methods</h2>
<h3>Scenario 1: Runnable but Not Sellable</h3>
<p>This is the most common situation. The creative is attractive, users click, but no conversions follow.</p>
<p><strong>Diagnosis Criteria</strong>:<br />
- Normal or high CTR, but extremely low CVR<br />
- High bounce rate, low add-to-cart rate<br />
- Converted user profiles differ significantly from target audience</p>
<p><strong>Likely Causes</strong>:<br />
A mismatch between landing page and creative messaging is the most frequent culprit. Users are attracted by one feature but discover the product actually emphasizes something else. Alternatively, user quality itself may be the issue—low-cost traffic often attracts price-sensitive users who click impulsively but never decide to purchase.</p>
<p><strong>Recommended Actions</strong>:<br />
Don't rush to pause the creative. First, use UTM parameters to trace user behavior paths after clicking to identify the drop-off point. If it's a landing page issue, prioritize optimizing page load speed and above-the-fold messaging alignment rather than simply testing new creatives. If it's an audience issue, narrow the audience or adjust interest targeting to exclude broad traffic.</p>
<h3>Scenario 2: Sellable but Not Runnable</h3>
<p>These creatives are more insidious. Conversion metrics look great, but limited reach prevents scaling.</p>
<p><strong>Diagnosis Criteria</strong>:<br />
- High CVR, decent ROI<br />
- Low CTR, elevated CPM<br />
- Below-target account spend</p>
<p><strong>Likely Causes</strong>:<br />
The problem lies in the creative's appeal itself. Strong sellability indicates solid product positioning, but the creative format may be too conventional, failing to stand out in the feed, or the copy may lack clarity, giving users insufficient incentive to click.</p>
<p><strong>Recommended Actions</strong>:<br />
Maintain existing creatives while testing 2-3 new versions following variable substitution principles—replace the opening three-second visual hook, use lookalike audiences for expansion, and consider loosening bidding strategy to give the system more opportunities to learn from high-converting user patterns.</p>
<h3>Scenario 3: Short-Term Runnable, Long-Term Unsellable</h3>
<p>This is a ticking time bomb. Early data will make you think you've found a winner, only to watch it bleed money over time.</p>
<p><strong>Diagnosis Criteria</strong>:<br />
- Normal CTR and CVR for the first 3-5 days<br />
- CVR steadily declining after day 7, CPA spiking<br />
- User feedback mentioning 'product doesn't match description'</p>
<p><strong>Likely Causes</strong>:<br />
The creative capitalized on a trending topic or short-term hook, attracting non-target users. Once novelty fades, traffic dries up, and user retention and LTV data will expose the problem.</p>
<p><strong>Recommended Actions</strong>:<br />
Set fixed observation checkpoints—for example, reviewing ROI trends every 72 hours. When CVR starts declining, immediately reduce budget rather than waiting for complete collapse. Begin building a creative pipeline to avoid gaps.</p>
<h2>Review Checklist: Differentiating the Two Creative Types</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Dimension</th>
<th>Runnable Creative</th>
<th>Sellable Creative</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Click Data</td>
<td>CTR ≥ industry average, low CPM</td>
<td>Moderate or low CTR</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Conversion Data</td>
<td>Low CVR, high CPA</td>
<td>High CVR, solid ROI</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>User Behavior</td>
<td>Short page dwell time, high bounce</td>
<td>High add-to-cart, high checkout</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Business Outcome</td>
<td>Many new users, low repeat purchase</td>
<td>High repeat rate, high LTV</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h2>Actionable Next Steps</h2>
<p>Returning to the opening case: what happened to that 8% CTR creative? The team used heatmapping tools and discovered 70% of user dwell time concentrated on the above-the-fold banner, with virtually no one scrolling down. The product's core selling point and the banner's displayed feature were completely mismatched.</p>
<p>After adjusting the above-the-fold messaging and narrowing the audience, conversion rate climbed from 0 to 3.2% within two weeks, with ROI reaching 1:4.5. The creative remained unchanged, but the funnel worked.</p>
<p>If your account has similar 'runnable but not sellable' creatives, don't rush to pause them. Tracing user behavior data to identify drop-off points is far more efficient than blindly testing new creatives.</p>
